WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:39.000
As the 2020 election cycle heats up, inevitably so will conversations among family, friends, and acquaintances on opposite ends of the political spectrum. The United States may be more politically polarized than ever, but our disagreements don't have to devolve into shouting matches and ideological one-upmanship. But how can you have a civil conversation about politics with someone who disagrees with you? Is it even worth it to try? For people who want to try to engage in meaningful dialogues with those who disagree with them. Psychologists have some practical advice to offer. Welcome to Speaking of Psychology, the flagship podcast of the American Psychological Association
随着2020年大选周期的升温，政治光谱对立的家人、朋友和熟人之间的对话也难免会随之升温。美国可能比以往任何时候都更加政治极化，但我们的分歧并不一定要演变成大喊大叫和意识形态上的争强好胜。但是，你如何能与一个与你意见不合的人就政治问题进行一场文明的对话呢？尝试一下真的值得吗？对于那些想要尝试与与自己意见不合的人进行有意义对话的人。心理学家有一些切实的建议。欢迎收听美国心理学会的旗舰播客《心理学畅谈》

2
00:00:39.000 --> 00:01:16.000
that explores the connections between psychological science and everyday life. I'm Kim Mills. Our guest today is Dr. Tanya Israel, a professor in the Department of Counseling, Clinical, and School Psychology at the University of California, Santa Barbara. after years of leading workshops teaching people how to talk about their political differences, she has published a book, Beyond Your Bubble, How to Connect Across the Political Divide, with advice on how to initiate these conversations, keep your cool, and ultimately better understand each other. Welcome to speaking of psychology, Dr. Israel.
该播客探讨了心理科学与日常生活之间的联系。我是金·米尔斯（Kim Mills）。我们今天的嘉宾是加利福尼亚大学圣巴巴拉分校咨询、临床与学校心理学系的教授托尼亚·伊斯雷尔博士（Dr. Tanya Israel）。经过多年领导工作坊，教导人们如何谈论他们的政治分歧后，她出版了一本名为《超越你的气泡：如何跨越政治鸿沟进行连接》（Beyond Your Bubble, How to Connect Across the Political Divide）的书，其中包含关于如何发起这些对话、保持冷静以及最终更好地彼此理解的建议。欢迎来到《心理学畅谈》，伊斯雷尔博士。

